  "title": "Kourovka problem 2.5 (main)",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "pdf-text-entry",
    "text": "According to Plotkin, a group is called an N R-group if the set of its nil-elements\ncoincides with the locally nilpotent radical, or, which is equivalent, if every inner\nautomorphism of it is locally stable. Can an N R-group have a nil-automorphism that\nis not locally stable?                                                   V. G. Vilyatser",
